To adjust the BGP keepalive interval to 60 seconds and ensure session stability, configure the following commands. These changes require coordinating with your peer to match the hold-time settings, as sessions will reset when applied.

### Global BGP Configuration (applies to all peers):
```cli
configure router
    bgp
        hold-time 180 min 180
        keepalive 60
    exit
exit
```

### Group-Level Configuration (applies to a specific peer group):
```cli
configure router
    bgp
        group "WAN_Peers"
            hold-time 180 min 180
            keepalive 60
        exit
    exit
exit
```

### Neighbor-Level Configuration (applies to a specific peer):
```cli
configure router
    bgp
        neighbor 192.0.2.1
            hold-time 180 min 180
            keepalive 60
        exit
    exit
exit
```

### Key Notes:
1. **`keepalive 60`**: Sets the BGP keepalive interval to 60 seconds.
2. **`hold-time 180 min 180`**:  
   - Configures the BGP hold-time to **180 seconds** (required to be ≥ 3 × keepalive interval).  
   - Enforces a **minimum hold-time of 180 seconds** to reject peer proposals with lower values, ensuring the session adheres to the 3:1 ratio (hold-time:keepalive).

### Critical Coordination:
- **Peer Agreement**: The peer must also support a hold-time of ≥180 seconds. Sessions will reset if peers propose incompatible timers.
- **Session Reset**: Changes trigger a BGP session reset. Schedule during maintenance windows.
- **Apply Context**: Use group/neighbor-level commands to target WAN peers specifically and avoid impacting other sessions.
